      "raw_completion": "{ \"goal_restatement\": \"Find all integers n such that the polynomial P(x) = x^n - 5x + 4 has exactly two distinct integer roots.\",\n\"conditions_complete\": true,\n\"contradictory\": false,\n\"multiple_reasonable_interpretations\": false,\n\"solution_exists\": true,\n\"unique_or_explicit_grading\": true,\n\"label\": \"A\",\n\"confidence\": 0.95,\n\"probability_label_A\": 0.95,\n\"issue_types\": [],\n\"reasoning_summary\": \"The polynomial P(x) = x^n - 5x + 4 is analyzed for integer roots. By the Rational Root Theorem, potential integer roots are factors of the constant term (4), which are \u00b11, \u00b12, and \u00b14. Testing these values, we find that x = 1 and x = 4 are roots. For P(x) to have exactly two distinct integer roots, n must be such that the polynomial can be factored as (x - 1)(x - 4)Q(x), where Q(x) is a polynomial of degree n-2. This implies n = 3. Thus, the only integer n that satisfies the condition is 3.\",\n\"derived_answer\":   \"}\"  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\